        "content": "<p>Hi all!</p>\n<p>Inspired by recent discussion on the noncomputability of polynomials in Mathlib, we’ve implemented a system that aims to automate proof of properties of univariate polynomials like degrees, leading coefficients, \"nth\" coefficients, and evaluations. Our tactics use type-class inference to construct a computable representation of the target property and then transform the current goal into an assertion about that computable representation. Those assertions can easily be resolved with last-mile tactics such as <code>trivial</code>, <code>simp</code>, or <code>norm_num</code>!</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Blueprint: <a href=\"https://liamschilling.github.io/AutomatePolynomial/blueprint/\">https://liamschilling.github.io/AutomatePolynomial/blueprint/</a></li>\n<li>Github: <a href=\"https://github.com/LiamSchilling/AutomatePolynomial\">https://github.com/LiamSchilling/AutomatePolynomial</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The project was developed for an undergraduate research position supervised by <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"246591\">@Quang Dao</span>, and we welcome feedback on our work.</p>\n<h3>Features</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>White-box automation with a Lean-native implementation</li>\n<li>Specific-purpose tactics that resolve goals with explicit polynomials</li>\n<li>Easily extensible to new representations for more efficient implementations—which could immediately be invoked by our tactics</li>\n<li>Certain systems modular to the target polynomial property, or the representation of that property</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Demonstrations</h3>\n<p><code>poly_rfl_degree_le</code> resolves greedy upper bounds on polynomial degree.</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">section</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">DegreeLe</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">variable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Semiring</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\">     </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"o\">])</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">degree</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≤</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⊥</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">poly_rfl_degree_le</span><span class=\"bp\">;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">trivial</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"w\">     </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"o\">])</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">degree</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≤</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">poly_rfl_degree_le</span><span class=\"bp\">;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">trivial</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"w\">     </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"o\">])</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">degree</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≤</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">poly_rfl_degree_le</span><span class=\"bp\">;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">trivial</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">^</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"o\">])</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">degree</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≤</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">poly_rfl_degree_le</span><span class=\"bp\">;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">trivial</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"o\">])</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">degree</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≤</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">poly_rfl_degree_le</span><span class=\"bp\">;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">trivial</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">end</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">DegreeLe</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p><code>poly_rfl_degree_eq_of_coeffs</code> resolves exact degrees by constructing a computable representation—<code>CoeffsList</code> in this case—of the entire polynomial.</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">section</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">DegreeEqOfCoeffs</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℕ</span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"o\">])</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">degree</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">poly_rfl_degree_eq_of_coeffs</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">VIA</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">CoeffsList</span><span class=\"bp\">;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">simp</span><span class=\"bp\">;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">trivial</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">end</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">DegreeEqOfCoeffs</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<h3>Future Directions</h3>\n<p>One obvious direction is generalizing the system for univariate polynomials into a system for multivariate polynomials. Some progress on this is available at <a href=\"https://github.com/LiamSchilling/AutomatePolynomial/tree/feature/mvpoly\">feature/mvpoly</a>.</p>\n<p>We are also investigating Lean's new <code>grind</code> tactic, which ships with a computable and somewhat automated representation of polynomials implemented by the <code>Poly</code> type. It's not entirely clear how those automations compare to ours, though future work may explore this question as well as if adopting the convenient <code>Poly</code> type could improve our system.</p>",
